<Project>

  <!--
    Applies to every project in the solution, production and test alike.

    RuntimeFrameworkVersion is what turns AC1's ".NET 10.0.11" into a stated, testable
    fact rather than an implication of whichever SDK happens to be installed. global.json
    pins the SDK band; this pins the runtime the output binds to. Gate A asserts both.

    What is NOT here any more: the coding-standard properties. Nullable,
    TreatWarningsAsErrors, ImplicitUsings, EnforceCodeStyleInBuild, AnalysisLevel,
    GenerateDocumentationFile and the CS1591 suppression all moved into
    Opinionated.DotNet.CodingStandards, declared as a GlobalPackageReference in
    Directory.Packages.props. The package sets each of them under a
    `Condition="'$(X)' == ''"` guard and Directory.Build.props is imported first, so
    anything restated here would silently win over the package - which defeats the point
    of having one. Restate a property here only to deliberately override the standard,
    and say why.

    Still true, and now the package's doing rather than this file's: invariants are
    "enforced by construction (type system, single choke point, lint/test gates) rather
    than by an agent remembering to read the spine". Note what is still absent: no
    code-coverage threshold. None appears in the PRD, the spine, the epics or the spec
    kernel, and the 80% figure in the test design is explicitly a proposal. Do not
    invent one.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeFrameworkVersion>10.0.11</RuntimeFrameworkVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Nothing else belongs here. Two ordering facts, both verified against
    `dotnet msbuild -preprocess`, decide where an override has to go:

      * This file is imported BEFORE the package's props, so a property set here wins over
        the package's `Condition="'$(X)' == ''"` default. That is how you override the
        standard - and why restating one of its values here is not a no-op but a silent
        fork of it.
      * The package's own properties are therefore NOT readable here. In particular
        ContinuousIntegrationBuild is set by the package, so a condition on it evaluates
        false in this file no matter the environment. An override that needs to read a
        package property belongs in Directory.Build.targets, which is imported after.
  -->

</Project>
